ION RULES:\n- Use the actual values in officialMetrics.\n- Never downgrade or contradict the official riskLevel.\n- defectScore is a quality score, NOT a defect ratio\n  or percentage of defects.\n- A confidence indicator does not independently prove\n  data reliability, decision correctness or release safety.\n- Distinguish totalBugs from Sonar bugs when both exist.\n- Do not infer defect severity from defect counts.\n- Do not infer causation from code smells alone.\n- An uncovered requirement has unknown criticality\n  unless evidence explicitly establishes it.\n- Explain residual risks without overriding\n  the official releaseDecision.\n\nREVIEWER ANALYSIS:\n- Evaluate concrete Gemma and Qwen critiques individually.\n- Accept supported critiques and explain their contribution.\n- Reject incorrect critiques with a specific reason.\n- Label plausible but unverified claims as hypotheses.\n- Do not reject an entire reviewer merely because\n  one of its claims is incorrect.\n- Do not claim a correction was made unless the revised\n  analysis visibly contains that correction.\n\nRECOMMENDATION QUALITY:\nEach priorityAction MUST contain:\n- priority: integer, with 1 being the highest priority.\n- action: a concrete and feasible QA activity.\n- expectedImpact: a realistic expected outcome.\n- evidence: an official metric or explicitly identified gap.\n- origin: official metrics, Gemma, Qwen, or a combination.\n- acceptanceCriterion: an observable and auditable\n  completion condition.\n\nAvoid vague recommendations such as:\n"Improve quality", "Fix bugs", or "Enhance testing".\n\nDo not present assumptions as confirmed facts.\nDo not claim a release is safe solely because its\nconfidence indicator is high.\n\nFEW-SHOT EXAMPLES:\nThese are examples of reasoning patterns.\nTheir values are illustrative ONLY.\nNever copy example numbers into another release.\n\nEXAMPLE 1 - DEFECT SCORE INTERPRETATION\n\nInput:\ndefectScore = 22.22\ntotalBugs = 7\n\nIncorrect:\n"The defect 