ION RULES:\n- Use the actual values in officialMetrics.\n- Never downgrade or contradict the official riskLevel.\n- defectScore is a quality score, NOT a defect ratio\n  or percentage of defects.\n- A confidence indicator does not independently prove\n  data reliability, decision correctness or release safety.\n- Distinguish totalBugs from Sonar bugs when both exist.\n- Do not infer defect severity from defect counts.\n- Do not infer causation from code smells alone.\n- An uncovered requirement has unknown criticality\n  unless evidence explicitly establishes it.\n- Explain residual risks without overriding\n  the official releaseDecision.\n\nREVIEWER ANALYSIS:\n- Evaluate concrete Gemma and Qwen critiques individually.\n- Accept supported critiques and explain their contribution.\n- Reject incorrect critiques with a specific reason.\n- Label plausible but unverified claims as hypotheses.\n- Do not reject an entire reviewer merely because\n  one of its claims is incorrect.\n- Do not claim a correction was made unless the revised\n  analysis visibly contains that correction.\n\nRECOMMENDATION QUALITY:\nEach priorityAction MUST contain:\n- priority: integer, with 1 being the highest priority.\n- action: a concrete and feasible QA activity.\n- expectedImpact: a realistic expected outcome.\n- evidence: an official metric or explicitly identified gap.\n- origin: official metrics, Gemma, Qwen, or a combination.\n- acceptanceCriterion: an observable and auditable\n  completion condition.\n\nAvoid vague recommendations such as:\n"Improve quality", "Fix bugs", or "Enhance testing".\n\nDo not present assumptions as confirmed facts.\nDo not claim a release is safe solely because its\nconfidence indicator is high.\n\nFEW-SHOT EXAMPLES:\nThese are examples of reasoning patterns.\nTheir values are illustrative ONLY.\nNever copy example numbers into another release.\n\nEXAMPLE 1 - DEFECT SCORE INTERPRETATION\n\nInput:\ndefectScore = 22.22\ntotalBugs = 7\n\nIncorrect:\n"The defect ratio of 22.22% indicates\nthat many critical bugs exist."\n\nCorrect:\n"The defect quality score is 22.22.\nThis is a quality indicator, not a defect\npercentage. Seven bugs are reported,\nbut their severity and business impact\nare not established by these metrics."\n\nEXAMPLE 2 - CONFIDENCE INTERPRETATION\n\nInput:\nconfidence = 95\nriskLevel = HIGH\nreleaseDecision = GO_WITH_RISK\n\nIncorrect:\n"Confidence of 95% proves that the\nrelease is safe and the data is reliable."\n\nCorrect:\n"The internal confidence indicator is 95.\nIt does not independently establish release\nsafety or data reliability. The official\nrisk level remains HIGH and the release\ndecision remains GO_WITH_RISK."\n\nEXAMPLE 3 - GENUINE SELF-CORRECTION\n\nOriginal analysis:\n"The main risk is moderate."\n\nOfficial metric:\nriskLevel = HIGH\n\nRevised analysis:\n"The official risk level is HIGH."\n\nCorrect correctedIssues:\n["Replaced the unsupported moderate-risk\ndescription with the official HIGH risk level."]\n\nIncorrect correctedIssues:\n["Recalculated defect ratio"]\n\nReason:\nNo defect-ratio recalculation occurred.\nA correction must be observable in the final analysis.\n\nEXAMPLE 4 - ACTIONABLE QA RECOMMENDATION\n\nInput:\ntotalBugs = 7\nDefect severity information is missing.\n\nIncorrect action:\n"Improve defect quality."\n\nCorrect priorityAction:\n{\n  "priority": 1,\n  "action": "Classify the 7 reported defects by\n             severity, status and business impact.",\n  "expectedImpact": "Provide evidence for\n                     release risk assessment.",\n  "evidence": "totalBugs = 7",\n  "origin": "Official metrics and reviewer critique",\n  "acceptanceCriterion": "All 7 defects have\n                          documented classifications\n                          or explicit information gaps."\n}\n\nFINAL SELF-CHECK:\nBefore producing the answer, verify that:\n1. Risk descriptions agree with official riskLevel.\n2. defectScore is never presented as a defect ratio.\n3. Confidence is not used as proof of release safety.\n4. Every claimed correction is visible in the output.\n5. Every priorityAction has an auditable criterion.\n6. Reviewer claims are critically assessed.\n7. Official releaseDecision remains unchanged.\n8. Missing evidence is clearly identified.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\nReturn ONLY one valid JSON object.\nNo Markdown, no comments, no explanatory text.\n\nThe JSON must have exactly these top-level keys:\n\n{\n  "executiveSummary": "string",\n  "releaseRationale": "string",\n  "topRisks": [\n    {\n      "dimension": "string",\n      "severity": "LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH|CRITICAL",\n      "reason": "string"\n    }\n  ],\n  "priorityActions": [\n    {\n      "priority": 1,\n      "action": "string",\n      "expectedImpact": "string",\n      "evidence": "string",\n      "origin": "string",\n      "acceptanceCriterion": "string"\n    }\n  ],\n  "managerMessage": "string",\n  "confidenceExplanation": "string",\n  "revision": {\n    "correctedIssues": ["string"],\n    "acceptedCritiques": ["string"],\n    "rejectedCritiques": ["string"],\n    "unverifiedClaims": ["string"],\n    "residualRisks": ["string"],\n    "requiredEvidence": ["string"]\n  }\n}\n\nAll revision arrays must contain concise strings.\nIf an array has no justified entries, return [].\n\nApply the rules to the actual input data.\nNever substitute illustrative example values\nfor officialMetrics values.\n	TASK: RECONSTRUCT THE QA ANALYSIS.\n\nThe previous Llama analysis contains factual errors.\nDo not reuse its sentences.\n\nSTEP 1 - IDENTIFY ERRORS\nExamine deterministicEvidenceChecks.\nTreat each confirmed contradiction as an error to remove.\n\nSTEP 2 - ANALYZE REVIEWER FEEDBACK\nExamine Gemma and Qwen independently.\nAccept or reject individual claims based on officialMetrics.\n\nSTEP 3 - RECONSTRUCT\nWrite a new executiveSummary, releaseRationale,\ntopRisks, priorityActions, managerMessage and\nconfidenceExplanation from officialMetrics.\n\nSTEP 4 - VERIFY\nCheck that no